This page sets up a block theme my-theme with one block. Plugins and child themes with code of their own are set up the same way; see Boot and modes, Plugins and Child themes for the differences.

You need PHP 8.5, Composer and Node 24 – or wp-dev-env, which provides them in a container together with a local WordPress.

Files

my-theme/
├── composer.json
├── functions.php
├── package.json
├── style.css
├── theme.json
├── templates/index.html
├── webpack.config.js
└── src/Block/Teaser/
    ├── Teaser.php
    ├── TeaserAttributes.php
    └── Teaser.twig

composer.json requires the framework from its GitHub repository by version and declares the PSR-4 namespace of the theme's classes in src/ and the settings of the release build:

{
    "name": "acme/my-theme",
    "type": "wordpress-theme",
    "repositories": [
        { "type": "vcs", "url": "https://github.com/saijsaij/wp-app-framework" }
    ],
    "require": {
        "php": ">=8.5",
        "saij/wp-app-framework": "^0.3"
    },
    "autoload": {
        "psr-4": { "Acme\\MyTheme\\": "src/" }
    },
    "scripts": {
        "build-manifest": "wp-app-framework build",
        "release": "wp-app-framework release"
    },
    "extra": {
        "strauss": {
            "target_directory": "vendor-prefixed",
            "namespace_prefix": "Acme\\MyTheme\\Vendor\\",
            "classmap_prefix": "Acme_MyTheme_Vendor_",
            "include_root_autoload": true,
            "update_call_sites": ["src", "functions.php"],
            "delete_vendor_packages": false
        }
    },
    "config": {
        "platform": { "php": "8.5" }
    }
}

style.css carries the theme header; Version: is the version of the release:

/*
Theme Name: My Theme
Version: 0.1.0
Requires at least: 7.1
Requires PHP: 8.5
Text Domain: my-theme
*/

functions.php loads the autoloader and boots the framework:

<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

// Release builds ship prefixed dependencies in vendor-prefixed/; source checkouts use Composer's vendor/.
require is_file(__DIR__ . '/vendor-prefixed/autoload.php')
    ? __DIR__ . '/vendor-prefixed/autoload.php'
    : __DIR__ . '/vendor/autoload.php';

Saij\WpAppFramework\Theme::boot();

package.json and webpack.config.js build the editor runtime of the framework and the editor scripts of the theme (see Editor). The slug is the directory name of the theme:

{
	"name": "my-theme",
	"private": true,
	"scripts": { "build": "wp-scripts build", "start": "wp-scripts start" },
	"devDependencies": {
		"@saij/wp-app-framework": "file:vendor/saij/wp-app-framework",
		"@wordpress/scripts": "^36.1.0"
	}
}

module.exports = require( './vendor/saij/wp-app-framework/webpack.config.js' )( {
	slug: 'my-theme',
	root: __dirname,
} );

theme.json and templates/index.html are those of any block theme.

A first block

A block is a service class with #[Block] and a render() method, an attribute class and a Twig template next to it (see Blocks):

<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Acme\MyTheme\Block\Teaser;

use Saij\WpAppFramework\Block\Block;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Block\BlockCategory;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Twig\TemplateRenderer;

#[Block('my-theme/teaser', title: 'Teaser', category: BlockCategory::Text, icon: 'text')]
final readonly class Teaser
{
    public function __construct(private TemplateRenderer $renderer) {}

    public function render(TeaserAttributes $attrs): string
    {
        return $this->renderer->render('Block/Teaser/Teaser.twig', ['attrs' => $attrs]);
    }
}

<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Acme\MyTheme\Block\Teaser;

use Saij\WpAppFramework\Block\PlainText;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Block\RichText;

final readonly class TeaserAttributes
{
    public function __construct(
        #[PlainText] public string $title = '',
        #[RichText(allowedFormats: ['core/bold', 'core/italic'])] public string $text = '',
        public bool $highlighted = false,
    ) {}
}

<section {{ block_wrapper_attributes({class: attrs.highlighted ? 'teaser teaser--highlighted' : 'teaser'}) }}>
    <h2{{ field('title') }}>{{ attrs.title }}</h2>
    <div{{ field('text') }}>{{ attrs.text|raw }}</div>
</section>

The title and the text are edited in the editor canvas; highlighted gets a toggle in the block inspector.

Build and run

composer install          # before npm: the file: dependency points into vendor/
npm install
npm run build             # editor runtime and editor scripts into build/
composer build-manifest   # checks attributes and services; writes nothing

Activate the theme in WordPress. In development the framework builds its manifest itself and caches it in wp-content/cache/my-theme/ (see Boot and modes); npm run start rebuilds the editor scripts on changes. For a release see Release.

A block is a service class with #[Block] and a render() method; its attributes are a typed attribute class. There is no block.json: the build derives the attribute schema from the attribute class, and WordPress passes it to the editor (get_block_editor_server_block_settings()). The post content stores the block comment with the attributes as JSON, e.g. <!-- wp:sections/hero {"topline":"Summer"} /-->. The only markup in the post content is the value of a #[RichText(postContent: true)] field: inline HTML without wrapping element, e.g. <!-- wp:sections/paragraph -->Summer <strong>party</strong><!-- /wp:sections/paragraph --> (see Text in the post content).

use Saij\WpAppFramework\Block\Align;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Block\Block;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Block\BlockCategory;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Block\Content;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Block\Image;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Block\Label;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Block\PlainText;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Block\RichText;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Block\Supports;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Twig\TemplateRenderer;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Value\ListOf;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Value\Url;

#[Block('sections/hero', title: 'Hero', category: BlockCategory::Design, icon: 'cover-image')]
#[Supports(anchor: true, align: [Align::Wide, Align::Full])]
final class Hero
{
    public function __construct(private readonly TemplateRenderer $renderer)
    {
    }

    public function render(HeroAttributes $attrs, string $content, WP_Block $block): string
    {
        return $this->renderer->render('Block/Hero/Hero.twig', ['attrs' => $attrs, 'content' => $content]);
    }
}

final readonly class HeroAttributes
{
    public function __construct(
        #[PlainText] public string $topline = '',
        #[RichText(allowedFormats: ['core/bold', 'core/italic'])] public string $headline = '',
        #[Image] #[Label('Image')] public ?int $imageId = null,
        public Gradient $gradient = Gradient::Black,
        #[ListOf(Button::class)] public array $buttons = [],
    ) {
    }
}

final readonly class Button
{
    public function __construct(
        #[PlainText] public string $label,
        #[Content] #[Url] public string $url = '',
    ) {
    }
}

<section {{ block_wrapper_attributes({class: 'hero hero--' ~ attrs.gradient.value}) }}>
    {% if attrs.topline or is_editor() %}
        <p{{ field('topline') }}>{{ attrs.topline }}</p>
    {% endif %}
    <h1{{ field('headline') }}>{{ attrs.headline|raw }}</h1>
    <figure{{ field('imageId') }}>{{ attachment_image(attrs.imageId, 'large') }}</figure>
    {% for button in attrs.buttons %}
        <a href="{{ button.url }}"{{ field(['buttons', loop.index0, 'label']) }}>{{ button.label }}</a>
    {% endfor %}
    <div{{ inner_blocks() }}>{{ content|raw }}</div>
</section>

field(), inner_blocks() and is_editor() only matter in the editor; on the front end the marks are empty (see Editor).

#[Block]

#[Block(name, title, category, icon: '', description: '', keywords: [], usesContext: [], allowedBlocks: null, excerptWrapper: false)] on a service class.

The name is namespace/name (lowercase letters, digits, -); the namespace core and duplicate names are build errors, and the framework itself declares no blocks.

category is a BlockCategory case.

usesContext lists the context values the block reads from WP_Block::$context, e.g. postId in a query loop.

allowedBlocks limits the blocks that can be inserted as inner blocks (allowed_blocks); it requires render() to accept string $content.

excerptWrapper adds a container to excerpt_allowed_wrapper_blocks (see Excerpts); it also requires string $content.

Blocks are registered on init with api_version 3.

#[Supports]

#[Supports(contentRole: false, anchor: false, align: [], customClassName: true, multiple: true, reusable: true, inserter: true, lock: true, renaming: true, customCSS: true)] with WordPress' defaults; only differing values are registered. html is always disabled. Container blocks set contentRole: true, so inner blocks can be inserted in contentOnly mode. customCSS (enabled by default in WordPress 7) lets users with edit_css store free CSS per block instance; the theme decides whether it allows it – per block with customCSS: false, or for all blocks with its own register_block_type_args hook.

render()

render() is public, non-static and returns string. Its parameters are matched by type, each at most once and in any order: the attribute class, string (the rendered inner blocks) and WP_Block. Services come through the constructor; the block service is created when the block is first rendered.

Attribute class

A value class (see Values) whose parameters all have a default value, because an attribute can always be missing in the content. The names lock, metadata, className, anchor, align and style are reserved for the attributes WordPress adds itself (style comes with the customCSS support, enabled by default in WordPress 7). The attribute schema gets type, enum, properties/items, default and role: content (below).

Fields

#[PlainText] (inline text, strings only), #[RichText(allowedFormats: null)] (inline HTML, strings only; allowedFormats lists the formats the editor offers, e.g. ['core/bold'], null allows all, [] none) and #[Image] (an attachment ID, ?int only) mark the fields edited in the canvas; #[Content] marks other content such as links.

With inspector: true (#[RichText(inspector: true)], also on #[PlainText] and #[Image]) a field is edited in the block inspector instead of the canvas – for values the markup does not show, or that are better edited in a form.

An attribute gets role: content if it or a nested field is marked, so the block stays editable in contentOnly mode (patterns and template parts open in it in WordPress 7). The marks have no effect on REST arguments.

Text in the post content

#[RichText(postContent: true)] stores the value as the block's inner HTML instead of in the block comment: inline HTML without wrapping element, so text blocks such as paragraphs and headings keep their text in the post content, while the markup stays in Twig (source: html without selector; the editor saves it with RawHTML). WordPress never parses sourced attributes on the server; the framework takes the value from the block content before creating the attribute object, so the template prints it like any other value ({{ attrs.text|raw }}).

Rules (build errors): on a parameter of the attribute class itself (not in value classes or list items), at most one per block, only in blocks without inner blocks (both would share the block content) and with the default '' (the editor stores an empty value as empty block content). The root element of the template may be the field itself, e.g. <p {{ block_wrapper_attributes() }}{{ field('text') }}>{{ attrs.text|raw }}</p>.

Excerpts

excerpt: true on #[PlainText] or #[RichText] (also in list items, value classes and with inspector: true) includes a value in automatic excerpts (get_the_excerpt() without a manual excerpt: archives, core/post-excerpt, feeds, REST). Blocks with such fields are added to excerpt_allowed_blocks.

WordPress renders allowed blocks for an excerpt while the get_the_excerpt filter runs and offers no other signal; during that filter the block renders only its excerpt fields, one paragraph each (rich text as stored, plain text escaped), without render() or its template – wp_trim_excerpt() strips the tags. If other code renders blocks inside a get_the_excerpt filter, framework blocks render as excerpts there, too.

WordPress never renders blocks with inner blocks for excerpts, so excerpt: true in such a block is a build error; #[Block(excerptWrapper: true)] includes a container's allowed inner blocks instead, like core/group.

Search is unchanged: WordPress searches the raw post content, which finds single words of values in the block comment JSON (stored as is, also non-ASCII), but no phrases across formatting and no values with &, <, > or " (stored as & etc.); attribute names and enum values match, too.

Editor labels

#[Label('…')] labels a value in the inspector and is the placeholder of an inline field; without it the label is derived from the parameter name (imageId → "Image id"). A backed enum implements Block\Labeled (label(): string, evaluated in the build) to label its cases; otherwise the editor shows the case values. #[Hidden] keeps a value out of the generated inspector (not allowed on inline fields).

Attributes at render time

WordPress validates the attributes against the schema before rendering (an invalid value falls back to the default) but does not sanitize them. The value factory sanitizes each attribute against its schema (rest_sanitize_value_from_schema()) and creates the attribute object; attributes of block supports are ignored.

Errors

Exceptions in render() or in creating the attributes are rethrown if WP_DEBUG is enabled; otherwise they are logged with error_log() and the block renders nothing.

Core blocks

The framework disables none. A theme decides itself which core blocks it allows, e.g. with a hook:

final class AllowedBlocks
{
    /**
     * @param bool|list<string> $allowed
     * @return bool|list<string>
     */
    #[Hook('allowed_block_types_all')]
    public function filter(bool|array $allowed): bool|array
    {
        return ['core/post-content', 'core/template-part', 'sections/hero' /* … */];
    }
}

Escaping

Value	Output
#[PlainText] and other strings	{{ attrs.topline }} – Twig's autoescaping
#[RichText]	{{ attrs.headline|raw }} – exactly as stored; WordPress filters it with wp_kses when a user without unfiltered_html saves
#[Url] strings	{{ button.url }} – the schema has format: uri, so the value is sanitized with sanitize_url() (disallowed protocols such as javascript: become empty) and then autoescaped
Images	#[Image] attachment ID with {{ attachment_image(attrs.imageId, size = 'full', attributes = {}) }} (wp_get_attachment_image(); empty for null)
Inner blocks	{{ content|raw }}
Root element	{{ block_wrapper_attributes(extra = {}) }} (get_block_wrapper_attributes(): classes and anchor of the supports)

No string filters such as upper on HTML (uppercase via CSS).

A child theme overrides templates of its parent theme in overrides/{parent-slug}/ (see Twig templates) and adjusts theme.json, block templates, template parts and patterns as WordPress allows; for that it needs no Composer, no boot and no build.

A child theme with code of its own – blocks, hooks, REST routes – is a framework instance like a plugin: its own composer.json (type: wordpress-theme, its style.css names the parent theme in Template:), its own PSR-4 namespace and block namespace, its own release with its own prefix, and ChildTheme::boot() in its functions.php (see Boot and modes). WordPress loads that file before the parent theme's; the instances share nothing. The build records the parent theme in the manifest, and the boot fails if another parent theme is active. Block names that collide with the parent's are errors, as between any two instances.

The child theme reaches its parent theme only through the parent's public interface (see Plugins): its public template callables, without declaring the parent, and its public API through a constructor parameter with #[ThemeApi] (Saij\WpAppFramework\Api\ThemeApi):

use My\Theme\Api\SectionsApi;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Api\ThemeApi;

final class Teasers
{
    public function __construct(
        #[ThemeApi]
        private readonly ?SectionsApi $sections = null,
    ) {}
}

The parent theme boots after the child theme, so the service must not be created before after_setup_theme. Require the parent theme as a development dependency for the IDE and static analysis. Child themes offer no public interface themselves.

use Saij\WpAppFramework\Hook\Hook;

final class TitleFilter
{
    #[Hook('the_title', priority: 20)]
    public function filter(string $title, Formatter $formatter, int $postId = 0): string
    {
        return $formatter->format($title, $postId);
    }
}

The method must be public and non-static and declare a return type: void registers an action, any other type a filter. #[Hook] is repeatable.

Parameters typed with a service candidate are injected (Formatter, see Services). All other parameters receive the WordPress arguments in their order ($title, $postId); accepted_args is derived from them. Missing optional arguments use the parameter default.

Services are created when the hook fires. Exceptions are rethrown if WP_DEBUG is enabled; otherwise they are logged with error_log(), and filters return their original value.

Plugins use the framework like themes (see Boot and modes) and may offer an interface to other consumers – the theme, other plugins. Themes may offer the same interface to their child themes. Instances never share PHP objects of the framework or of its dependencies: each release prefixes them with its own namespace. The interface therefore consists of WordPress hooks named after the provider's slug and of signatures that use only PHP, WordPress and the provider's own classes; the build of the provider rejects other types (also in the project classes such a signature uses). The provider keeps its interface compatible itself.

Public API

#[PublicApi] (Saij\WpAppFramework\Api\PublicApi) marks a service of a plugin or theme as its API. A consumer receives the API of a plugin through a constructor parameter:

use My\Plugin\Api\NoteApi;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Api\PluginApi;

final class Notes
{
    public function __construct(
        #[PluginApi('my-plugin')]
        private readonly ?NoteApi $notes = null,
    ) {}
}

The parameter must be nullable with the default null; it is null if the plugin is not active. The build does not load the class, so the plugin may be missing; require it as a development dependency (require-dev) for the IDE and static analysis – the release build installs without development dependencies. The service is created when the consumer's service is created; doing that before plugins_loaded is an error, and so is an active plugin that does not provide the class.

Public template functions and filters

#[TemplateFunction(name, public: true)] and #[TemplateFilter(name, public: true)] in a plugin or theme offer the callable to the templates of other consumers. The name must start with the provider's template prefix – its slug with underscores, e.g. my_plugin_ for my-plugin. A consumer lists the plugins it uses in its composer.json and calls the callables by name (a child theme uses those of its parent theme without listing it):

"extra": { "wp-app-framework": { "template-plugins": ["my-plugin"] } }

{% if has_plugin('my-plugin') %}
    {{ my_plugin_note_count() }} {{ title|my_plugin_highlight }}
{% endif %}

Twig resolves names while compiling templates, so a name with a declared prefix compiles without the plugin; it is resolved when called, and calling it without the plugin is an error – check with has_plugin(slug). Arguments are positional. Results of html: true callables are printed unescaped; the input of such a filter is escaped first unless it is the result of an HTML function or filter (unlike filters of the own instance, a value marked with |raw is escaped as well). Names starting with a declared prefix are reserved; the consumer's own callables must not use them.

Template overrides

A theme overrides templates of a plugin in overrides/{plugin-slug}/; see Twig templates.

use Saij\WpAppFramework\Rest\Format;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Rest\HttpMethod;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Rest\Param;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Rest\Permission\Callback;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Rest\Permission\Capability;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Rest\Permission\PublicAccess;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Rest\RestController;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Rest\RestException;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Rest\RestRoute;

#[RestController('sections/v1', base: '/items')]
final class ItemController
{
    #[RestRoute('/{id}', HttpMethod::Get, new PublicAccess())]
    public function show(int $id, ItemRepository $items, Variant $variant = Variant::Short): array
    {
        return $items->find($id) ?? throw new RestException('sections_item_not_found', 'Item not found.', 404);
    }

    #[RestRoute('/{id}', [HttpMethod::Put, HttpMethod::Patch], new Callback(self::canEdit(...)))]
    public function update(int $id, #[Param(maxLength: 200)] string $title, #[Param(format: Format::Email)] ?string $contact = null): WP_REST_Response
    {
        // …
        return new WP_REST_Response($data, 201);
    }

    #[RestRoute('/{id}', HttpMethod::Delete, new Capability('delete_posts'))]
    public function delete(int $id): void
    {
        // … responds with 204 No Content
    }

    public static function canEdit(int $id, ItemRepository $items): bool
    {
        return current_user_can('edit_post', $items->getPostId($id));
    }
}

Controller and routes

#[RestController(namespace, base: '')] on the class defines the REST namespace (no leading or trailing slash) and an optional base path prepended to every route of the class. The value may be a class constant. A #[RestRoute] in a class without it is a build error.

#[RestRoute(path, methods, permission)] is repeatable; all three arguments are required, so a missing permission is reported by the IDE and by the build. path is empty or starts with /; methods is an HttpMethod case or a list of them.

Permission

new PublicAccess() (everybody), new Capability('…') (current_user_can()) or new Callback(self::method(...)). A callback is a first-class callable of a public static method returning bool – PHP allows first-class callables in attribute arguments only for static methods. Its parameters follow the route rules below; request arguments must exist in the route with the same name and type. Before calling it, the framework validates and sanitizes these arguments against the route's own schema and denies access if one is invalid, because WordPress also calls permission callbacks for the Allow header with a request validated for another handler.

Path placeholders

{name} matches the parameter of the same name, which must be required and of type int (\d+), string ([^/]+) or a backed enum (its values). Their values are taken from the URL parameters, never from a query argument of the same name. Route paths may only contain letters, digits, ., _, ~, -, / and placeholders.

Parameters

Service candidates are injected, WP_REST_Request receives the request, all other parameters are request arguments of the same name: int, float, string, bool, a backed enum or array with #[ListOf(Enum::class)] / #[ListOf(ValueClass::class)] (see Values), optionally nullable with a default value. A single value class without #[ListOf] is not supported: a class type is injected as service.

The argument schema (type, enum, items, required, default) is derived from the signature; WordPress validates and sanitizes the arguments, answers 400 on errors and shows the schema on OPTIONS. Missing optional arguments use the parameter default; enums, value objects and lists are converted by the value factory.

#[Param(description:, minimum:, maximum:, minLength:, maxLength:, pattern:, format: Format::…)] adds schema keywords; the build checks that they apply to the parameter type.

Responses and errors

Responses: a void method responds with 204 No Content; any other value goes through rest_ensure_response() (200). Return a WP_REST_Response for another status or headers.

Errors: throw RestException(code, message, status, data) (status 400–599) for an error response. Any other exception is logged with error_log() and answered with 500 wp_app_framework_internal_error; class, message, file, line and stack trace are included only if WP_DEBUG is enabled.

Services are created when the route is called.

Templates live in the src/ directory of each level, next to the code that uses them (src/Hook/Mail.twig) or in shared directories (src/Component/Button.twig). A template name is always the full path relative to src/, including the extension – in PHP and in templates alike; there are no relative names.

use Saij\WpAppFramework\Hook\Hook;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Twig\TemplateFilter;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Twig\TemplateFunction;
use Saij\WpAppFramework\Twig\TemplateRenderer;

final class Mail
{
    public function __construct(private readonly TemplateRenderer $renderer)
    {
    }

    #[Hook('sections_mail_body')]
    public function body(string $body, string $name): string
    {
        return $this->renderer->render('Hook/Mail.twig', ['body' => $body, 'name' => $name]);
    }
}

final class Formatting
{
    #[TemplateFunction('price')]
    public function price(int $cents): string
    {
        return number_format($cents / 100, 2, ',', '.') . ' €';
    }

    #[TemplateFilter('highlight', html: true)]
    public function highlight(string $value): string
    {
        return '<mark>' . $value . '</mark>';
    }
}

{% include 'Component/Button.twig' %}      {# child overrides → theme → framework #}
{% include '@framework/Component/Button.twig' %} {# exactly this level #}
{{ price(total) }} {{ name|highlight }}

Lookup

Names without a namespace are searched in overrides/{theme-slug}/ of the active child theme (if it has that directory), then in the theme's src/, then in the framework's. A higher level overrides a template by providing the same path: a child theme overrides src/Block/Hero/Hero.twig of the theme my-theme with overrides/my-theme/Block/Hero/Hero.twig. @theme/… and @framework/… address exactly one level and cannot be overridden – use them where the exact template matters, e.g. when an override extends the original. wp-app-framework build lists templates that override framework templates.

Child themes: the src/ of a child theme belongs to its own framework instance (if it boots one): its templates are searched in its src/, then in the framework's, and never override templates of the theme.

Plugins: a plugin's templates are searched in overrides/{plugin-slug}/ of the child theme and of the theme, then in the plugin's src/, then in the framework's. So a theme overrides src/Block/Note/Note.twig of the plugin my-plugin with overrides/my-plugin/Block/Note/Note.twig; its own templates in src/ never collide with plugin templates. @plugin/… addresses the plugin's own templates exactly. Overrides are rendered by the plugin's Twig environment: the plugin's template functions and filters and the context the plugin passes apply.

Instances: compiled templates are named after the slug of their instance as well, so a theme and a plugin never share the compiled code of templates with the same path.

Environment

Autoescaping (html) and strict_variables are always on; undefined variables are errors. In development mode, debug, auto reload and dump() are enabled.

Compiled templates are cached in wp-content/cache/{slug}/twig/. Release mode compiles without auto reload, so each release uses its own directory wp-content/cache/{slug}/twig/{templateVersion}/; the version is a hash over the names and contents of all templates of the project and the framework, written into the release manifest by build --output. If the active theme or child theme overrides templates of another instance (a plugin, or the theme overridden by its child theme), that instance's directory gets a further level named after the versions of theme and child theme: a changed override needs a new theme version. Directories of older releases are not removed yet.

Twig exceptions reach the caller, so the error handling of hooks and REST routes applies.

Template functions and filters

#[TemplateFunction(name, html: false)] / #[TemplateFilter(name, html: false)] register public, non-static methods of services; the service is created when a template first calls them. Names must be valid identifiers, unique per kind and must not reuse a function or filter Twig provides (Twig would silently replace it); parameters must not be services (inject them through the constructor), variadic or by reference. A filter receives the filtered value as its first parameter.

Escaping: results are escaped unless html: true; such a method must return string. An HTML filter receives the filtered value HTML-escaped unless it is already safe (e.g. |raw, like Twig's nl2br). Other arguments are passed unescaped: an HTML function or filter must escape every argument it puts into its result.

Callables offered to other consumers (public: true) are described in Plugins.
